[FINAL-TEXT] The root cause is confirmed. The BIND configuration on `srv1` explicitly has:

```
recursion no;
```

The comment in the file even describes it: *"Offline lab: no upstream, so recursion is off and the server answers its own zone only."* This is the injected fault — recursion was disabled, leaving srv1 as a strictly authoritative-only DNS server. PC1 (10.1.0.100/24) points to 10.5.0.4 as its sole nameserver. When any external hostname query is made (google.com, etc.), `named` returns `REFUSED` because it won't recurse. This means browsers cannot resolve any internet hostnames → pages spin and time out. ICMP to internet IPs works perfectly because it doesn't need DNS.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The BIND DNS daemon (`named`) on srv1 (10.5.0.4), specifically the `recursion no;` directive in `/etc/bind/named.conf`.

**Mechanism:** srv1 acts as the sole DNS resolver for all Office-A hosts (PC1's `/etc/resolv.conf` lists `nameserver 10.5.0.4`). With `recursion no;` set, BIND operates in authoritative-only mode and refuses to perform recursive lookups for any domain it does not directly host. Queries for external internet hostnames (e.g., `google.com`) receive an immediate `REFUSED` / "recursion not available" response. Web browsers depend on DNS name resolution before opening TCP connections, so every external web page stalls indefinitely — exactly the "spins and times out" symptom reported by the user.

**Localization:** srv1 (IP 10.5.0.4, interface eth0 on the 10.5.0.0/24 VLAN-30 segment in Office B). The BIND process is alive and listening on UDP/TCP port 53; the server is reachable with 0% ICMP loss; the fault is purely in the DNS recursion policy. All other layers are healthy: L3 routing, OSPF fabric, NAT on r1, and the physical/virtual links are all clean.

**Root cause:** `recursion no;` was added to the `options {}` block of `/etc/bind/named.conf` on srv1, disabling recursive query resolution for all clients including Office-A PCs that rely on srv1 as their resolver.

**Fix:** On srv1, change `recursion no;` to `recursion yes;` (and optionally scope it with `allow-recursion { any; };` or the internal subnets), then reload BIND with `rndc reload` or `systemctl reload named`.
=== END ===